INT. FINCH & PUMPHREY TAXIDERMY SHOP - DAY

Glass eyes everywhere. A moth-eaten badger on a pedestal. A stuffed owl in a bowler hat.

GLORIA FINCH, 40s, sharp as a tack in a sharper suit, stands on a stepladder, hammering a mounted trout to the wall.

WALTER PUMPHREY, 50s, rumpled, hat in both hands, bursts through the door.

WALTER
Gloria! The fire inspector is outside!

GLORIA
(not turning)
Good. Tell him we're closed.

WALTER
He saw the smoke!

GLORIA
There is no smoke. There is a very warm badger.

She hammers. He wrings his hat. She hammers harder.

WALTER
Gloria, you put a lit cigar in its mouth!

GLORIA
To keep it from looking surprised.

He snatches the badger. It's smoking. He bats at it with his hat. Smoke curls from the badger's ears.

WALTER
Put it out!

GLORIA
I can't, I'm busy with the trout.

He dumps a bucket of paint water on the badger. It sags. She climbs down, sniffs, turns slowly, and lets him have a look.

GLORIA (CONT'D)
That was my best badger.

WALTER
He was a fire hazard!

She picks up the owl. Plops it on his head.

GLORIA
Well, put this on. You're the one who has to face the inspector.

WALTER
Why me?

GLORIA
He asked for the owner.

WALTER
You own half!

GLORIA
And you are the half with the hat.

The door chimes. They both freeze. She straightens his bowler. He straightens the owl.

WALTER
How do I look?

GLORIA
Like you've been stuffed for years.

The door swings open.
